        <h1>Therapy for <em>Veterans &amp; Military Families</em><span class="h1-loc">Pasadena, TX &middot; In-person &amp; statewide telehealth</span></h1>
        <p class="lead">A direct, no-nonsense approach from a clinician who has worn the uniform. Serving veterans, service members, and their families across Texas — in Pasadena and statewide via telehealth. TRICARE and ChampVA may be covered — reach out to verify.</p>
        <div class="button-row">
          <a class="btn btn-gold" href="/intake.html">Book a Free Consultation</a>
          <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="#what-we-do">What Therapy Looks Like</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- INTRO -->
    <section class="section" id="intro">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="what-is-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1.15fr .85fr; gap: 64px; align-items: start;">
          <div class="reveal prose">
            <span class="eyebrow">You're Not "Broken"</span>
            <h2>Therapy from someone who understands the culture.</h2>
            <p>The transition out of a mission-driven environment is rarely clean. The structure, the purpose, the people who had your back — and then a civilian world that doesn't quite get it. You may be carrying things you've never said out loud: what you saw, what you did, what it cost, and the quiet pressure of holding it all together for everyone else.</p>
            <p>I'm <strong>David</strong>, a Licensed Psychotherapist and a U.S. Army veteran. I built this practice in Pasadena, Texas around the people I understand best: veterans, active and reserve service members, and the spouses and families who serve alongside them. You won't have to translate military life into civilian terms here, or explain why "just relax" was never going to cut it.</p>
            <p>Whether you're dealing with combat-related trauma, the grind of reintegration, anger that shows up faster than it used to, sleep that won't come, or a marriage that's strained by deployments and distance — this is work we can do together. And if it's not for you, it may be for someone close to you: see <a class="inline-link" href="mens-mental-health.html">men's mental health</a> or <a class="inline-link" href="couples-therapy.html">couples therapy</a>.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="reveal">
            <div class="quote-card">
              <p>"I spent years telling myself I was fine because other guys had it worse. I didn't want a lecture — I wanted to talk to someone who actually got it."</p>
              <small>What veterans often say before they start</small>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-top: 22px;">
              <p style="color: var(--muted); font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 12px;">Therapy here is not:</p>
              <div class="myth-busted">
                <span class="myth-tag">A weakness</span>
                <span class="myth-tag">A career risk</span>
                <span class="myth-tag">Reliving everything</span>
                <span class="myth-tag">Only for combat vets</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- WHAT THERAPY LOOKS LIKE -->
    <section class="section dark-section" id="what-we-do">
      <div class="container">
        <span class="eyebrow">How The Work Moves</span>
        <h2>Evidence-based methods, applied to real life.</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">I use structured, well-researched approaches — not open-ended talk that goes nowhere. We start with what's getting in the way right now, build practical tools, and keep the work tied to the decisions and relationships that actually matter to you. Three methods anchor the work:</p>
        <div class="cards-3">
          <article class="card reveal">
            <span class="num">CBT</span>
            <h3>Break the cycle</h3>
            <p class="method-sub">Cognitive Behavioral Therapy</p>
            <p>When your mind keeps replaying it — always scanning, always on guard — we find what's driving that and build real ways to interrupt it. Practical skills you can actually use, not just talk.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="card reveal">
            <span class="num">DBT</span>
            <h3>Steady yourself when it hits hard</h3>
            <p class="method-sub">Dialectical Behavior Therapy</p>
            <p>Concrete tools for the moments that come on fast — anger, panic, the urge to shut down or numb out. Ways to ride it out and stay grounded instead of getting hijacked.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="card reveal">
            <span class="num">ACT</span>
            <h3>Carry it without it running you</h3>
            <p class="method-sub">Acceptance &amp; Commitment Therapy</p>
            <p>Some things can't just be argued away — grief, moral injury, the weight of what you carry from service. We make room for the hard stuff while you keep moving toward the people and life that matter.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- WHAT WE WORK ON -->
    <section class="section" style="background: var(--cream);">
      <div class="container">
        <span class="eyebrow">Common Reasons Vets Reach Out</span>
        <h2>What we work on together.</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">Every story is different, but these are the themes that bring veterans and military families to therapy most often. You don't need a diagnosis or a "big enough" reason to start.</p>
        <div class="light-cards-3">
          <div class="light-card reveal">
            <small>Trauma &amp; PTSD</small>
            <h3>Combat &amp; Service Stress</h3>
            <p>Intrusive memories, hypervigilance, startle response, nightmares, and the feeling of never fully standing down. We work to help your nervous system register that the threat has passed.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="light-card reveal">
            <small>Reintegration</small>
            <h3>Transition to Civilian Life</h3>
            <p>Loss of mission and identity, a workplace that feels foreign, and the question of who you are now. We rebuild structure, purpose, and direction for this chapter.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="light-card reveal">
            <small>Anger &amp; Irritability</small>
            <h3>A Shorter Fuse</h3>
            <p>When frustration shows up faster and louder than it used to — at home, on the road, at work. We get underneath the anger to what it's protecting, and build steadier responses.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="light-card reveal">
            <small>Moral Injury &amp; Grief</small>
            <h3>What You Carry</h3>
            <p>The weight of decisions made under impossible conditions, survivor's guilt, and losses that never got room to be grieved. Space to set it down without judgment.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="light-card reveal">
            <small>Family &amp; Marriage</small>
            <h3>Strain on Loved Ones</h3>
            <p>Deployments, distance, and the ripple effects of service on a spouse and kids. Support for the veteran and for the family carrying it alongside them.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="light-card reveal">
            <small>Sleep &amp; Numbing</small>
            <h3>Coping That Stopped Working</h3>
            <p>Insomnia, drinking more than you mean to, staying constantly busy, or going numb. We replace the survival strategies that have outlived their usefulness.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- INSURANCE & RATES -->
    <section class="section" id="insurance">
      <div class="container">
        <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: .9fr 1.1fr; gap: 56px; align-items: start;" class="what-is-grid">
          <div class="reveal">
            <span class="eyebrow">Insurance &amp; Rates</span>
            <h2>TRICARE &amp; ChampVA welcome here.</h2>
            <p class="section-lead">Coverage shouldn't be the reason you put this off. As a veteran-owned practice, I prioritize military benefits and keep the process simple. If you don't see your plan, reach out — we'll check together before your first session.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="reveal insurance-panel">
            <h3 style="margin-bottom: 16px;">Plans accepted</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 13px; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: .04em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--gold); margin-bottom: 8px;">Military &amp; Veteran Coverage</p>
            <div class="plan-chips">
              <span class="plan-chip lead">TRICARE East</span>
              <span class="plan-chip lead">TRICARE for Life</span>
              <span class="plan-chip lead">ChampVA</span>
